<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
  Imported automatically by every project under this folder, which is the point: the version
  is stated once and the seven projects inherit it. MSBuild walks up from each .csproj until
  it finds this file, so a new project picks it up by existing.

  BUMPING A RELEASE TOUCHES TWO MORE FILES, and nothing here can reach them:

      src\AddIn.V20\Config.xml     <AddInVersion> and <Product><Version>
      src\AddIn.V21\Config.xml     the same

  The Publisher's schema wants the number written out in its own XML, and it is what TIA
  shows in Options -> Add-Ins. Leaving those behind produces an Add-In that reports one
  version while the assemblies inside it carry another - which is worse than no version at
  all, because the report a tester sends back is then wrong rather than missing.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      One number, and only one. The SDK derives the rest and they are deliberately not
      restated here: AssemblyVersion and FileVersion both come out as 1.0.0.0, verified by
      building with nothing but this property set.

      They are three digits against four because they are two different things.
      Version is SemVer - three components plus an optional suffix, 1.0.0-beta.1 - and it
      feeds AssemblyInformationalVersion. AssemblyVersion and FileVersion are System.Version,
      exactly four integers with no room for a suffix, so a three-part number is padded.

      Restating all three would recreate the drift this file exists to remove: raise Version
      alone and the assemblies would keep reporting the old number, silently.
    -->
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>

    <Product>PLC-Framework</Product>
    <Company>PLC-Framework</Company>
    <Authors>cyanezf (PLC-Framework)</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 PLC-Framework</Copyright>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
